.TH MKFS.FAT 8
.SH NAME
mkfs.fat \- construct a FAT16 or FAT32 filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkfs.fat
[
.B \-Nv
] [
.B \-F
.I 16|32
] [
.B \-i
.I volume-id
] [
.B \-n
.I label
] [
.B \-s
.I sectors-per-cluster
]
.I special
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfs.fat
creates an empty FAT16 or FAT32 filesystem on the block device or regular
image file named by
.IR special .
The target must not be mounted.
Its size is obtained from the disk driver or the regular file length.
.PP
The formatter uses 512-byte sectors, two FAT copies, and a compact streaming
write path suitable for small ReBSD systems.
FAT16 is selected automatically below 512 MiB and FAT32 at or above 512 MiB.
FAT12 is not supported.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-F 16|32
Select the FAT type explicitly.
.TP
.B \-i volume-id
Set the 32-bit volume identifier.  The value may use decimal or C-style
hexadecimal notation.
.TP
.B \-n label
Set a volume label of at most 11 DOS-compatible characters.
Lowercase ASCII letters are converted to uppercase.
.TP
.B \-s sectors-per-cluster
Set the cluster size in sectors.  The value must be a power of two from 1
through 128 and must produce a valid cluster count for the selected FAT type.
.TP
.B \-N
Print the selected geometry without writing the filesystem.
.TP
.B \-v
Print additional geometry information.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means the filesystem was created successfully.
One means the parameters or target were invalid, or an I/O error occurred.
.SH SEE ALSO
fdisk(8), fsck.fat(8), mkfs(8), mkfs.ufs(8), mount(8)
